.TH "YES" 7 "November 2025" "atomdns handlers" "atomdns authors"

.SH "NAME"
.PP
\fIyes\fP \- always respond to positively to queries

.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
This is used to have an "empty" zone delegated to a server and have it positively answer to any query it
gets, thereby not giving away your zone's content.

.PP
A major use case of this handler is to respond to queries from certificate authorities that want to perform
a DNS lookup. Note this is \fIdifferent\fP than the
DNS\-01
\[la]https://letsencrypt.org/docs/challenge-types/#dns-01-challenge\[ra] challenge that wants to resolve a
TXT record with a specific value in it. The aim is to have the most \fIminimal\fP setup that makes this work for
all potential names in the zone.

.PP
For A and AAAA queries the \fB\fCsource\fR property is used, it returns the IPv4 address for A and IPv6 address for
AAAA queries. The default nameserver for this zone is \fB\fCns.<zone>\fR, but see the \fB\fCns\fR property if you need
extra server to be configured.

.PP
This handler handles A, AAAA, NS, SOA and CAA queries, all others will return a NODATA response.

.SH "SYNTAX"
.PP
.RS

.nf
yes {
    caa VALUE
    source IP|IFACE [IP|IFACE]
    ns NAMESERVER
}

.fi
.RE

.IP \(bu 4
\fB\fCcaa\fR hold CAA's record \fBVALUE\fP. This may be repeated and will result in multiple CAA records to be returned.
.IP \(bu 4
With \fB\fCsource\fR you must specify the server's local addresses, this is to return correct responses for A and
AAAA queries coming in over IPv6 and IPv4 respectively. You can use an interface name as \fBIFACE\fP and the
routable IP address from the interface are also used. If this yields multiple addresses per family only the
first are used.
.IP \(bu 4
The optional \fB\fCns\fR can be used to add extra \fBNAMESERVER\fPs that are returned for NS queries. These servers
should be out of bailiwick.


.SH "EXAMPLES"
.PP
.RS

.nf
example.org {
    log
    yes {
        caa letsencrypt.org
        source eth0
    }
}

.fi
.RE

.PP
Allows names under example.org to have TLS certificates generated by Lets Encrypt. This will generate a record
like so: \fB\fCwww.example.org. IN CAA 128 issue "letsencrypt.org"\fR. All routable address of the \fB\fCeth0\fR interface
are used to answer address queries.

.SH "NOTES"
.PP
This handler came into existence because some certificate authorities have ridicules requirements for TLS
certificate creation with respect to DNS. Also note that this handler simplifies things, but there can't be
anything wrong with the delegation (or setup) of this server. I.e. a service like dnsviz
\[la]https://dnsviz.net/\[ra]
must return "all green" when you are testing a name. This means the parent delegation to a zone served by
\fIyes\fP needs at least the \fB\fCns.<zone>\fR server, plus the A (and potentially) AAAA glue that is need for an
in\-bailiwick delegation.

.SH "BUGS"
.PP
DNSSEC is not implemented.

.SH "ALSO SEE"
.PP
yes(1).
